          "subtype": "QUESTION",
          "title": "Example-1",
          "tags": [
            "EXERCISE"
          ],
          "question": "Classify the following amine as Primary, Secondary or Tertiary-",
          "answer": "Tertiary (3°) Amine.\n\nIn the given compound, the nitrogen atom is directly bonded to:\n\n  • Two methyl groups (-CH3)\n  • An aromatic ring\nTherefore, the nitrogen atom is directly bonded to three carbon groups.\n\nHence, the given amine is classified as a Tertiary (3°) Amine.",
          "prompt": "Classify the following amine as Primary, Secondary or Tertiary-",
          "options": []
        },
        {
          "id": "402aac1a-1a35-44e1-8d2e-276e485cded1",
          "index": 4,
          "subtype": "QUESTION",
          "title": "Example-2",
          "tags": [
            "EXERCISE"
          ],
          "question": "Melting points are normally the highest for\n\n(1) Tertiary amides\n\n(2) Secondary amides\n\n(3) Primary amides\n\n(4) Amines",
          "answer": "Ans.  (3) Primary amides\n\nAnswer: (3) Primary amides\n\nMelting points are generally the highest for primary amides because of the extensive hydrogen bonding between the amide molecules. The primary amides\n\n($ -CONH_2−​$) have both $ -NH_2−​$and carbonyl groups, allowing them to form strong intermolecular hydrogen bonds, which require more energy to break.\n\nTherefore, primary amides have maximum melting points.",
          "options": [
            "(1) Tertiary amides",
            "(2) Secondary amides",
            "(3) Primary amides",
            "(4) Amines"
          ],
          "prompt": "Melting points are normally the highest for"
        },
        {
          "id": "9321ad77-2a56-4e66-af60-2a34a0a66d70",
          "index": 5,
          "subtype": "TEXT",
          "title": "Common Mistake",
          "tags": [
            "COMMON_CONFUSIONS",
            "beginner",
            "theory",
            "K-12",
            "Concept Explanation"
          ],
          "text": "• Confusing primary, secondary, and tertiary amines.\n  • Misunderstanding the basicity of amines and their comparison with ammonia.\n  • Forgetting to consider the effect of substituents on amine basicity.\n  • Incorrectly assigning IUPAC names or structural representations."
        }
      ]
    },
